  "function_translated_nl": "Lesson context: lesson_08. Let triangle ABC be a equilateral isosceles triangle where \u2220A = 60\u00b0, \u2220B = 60\u00b0, \u2220C = 60\u00b0. Let triangle DEF be a equilateral isosceles triangle with sides DE = 12, EF = 12, DF = 12, where \u2220D = 60\u00b0, \u2220E = 60\u00b0, \u2220F = 60\u00b0. Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF by SSS similarity with scale factor 1.5. Find the length of AB in ABC.",
  "student_facing_prose": "A jeweler is designing two equilateral triangular pendants. The first pendant, triangle ABC, will be created by scaling up a second pendant, triangle DEF, using a scale factor of 1.5.\n\nThe second pendant, triangle DEF, is equilateral with all sides measuring DE = 12, EF = 12, and DF = 12. Since it's equilateral, all three angles measure 60\u00b0: \u2220D = 60\u00b0, \u2220E = 60\u00b0, and \u2220F = 60\u00b0.\n\nThe first pendant, triangle ABC, is also equilateral with all angles measuring 60\u00b0 each: \u2220A = 60\u00b0, \u2220B = 60\u00b0, and \u2220C = 60\u00b0. Because triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF by SSS similarity with scale factor 1.5, all corresponding sides of ABC are 1.5 times as long as the sides of DEF.\n\nHow long should each side of the larger pendant ABC be?"
}